WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:05.250
In Britain, the words "rail replacement bus"
are not good words to hear.

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:07.940
It means the train you wanted to take isn't
going anywhere,

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:11.099
and instead everyone is going to have to get
on a slow and lumbering bus

00:00:11.099 --> 00:00:14.969
that will take at least twice as long and
be so much less comfortable.

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:17.090
If you've already left the first station,

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:20.350
then the rail company is required to get you
to your destination...

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:22.890
but there's no rule to say how fast.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:26.850
Rail replacement buses are the
train traveller's nightmare.

00:00:26.850 --> 00:00:29.780
Which is why I was surprised to learn that,
for just a few days,

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.750
things were happening the opposite way around:

00:00:31.750 --> 00:00:34.149
here, between two remote villages
in the lowlands of Scotland,

00:00:34.149 --> 00:00:39.089
there is a very rare sight:
the bus replacement train.

00:00:39.089 --> 00:00:45.030
The normal schedule here is to run every weekend,
usually from Easter to the end of September.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:49.019
The bus replacement service was an idea that
was born out of some discussions

00:00:49.019 --> 00:00:52.829
between some villagers and the volunteers
on the railway,

00:00:52.829 --> 00:00:56.719
and then once we realised there would be enough
volunteers to run this week,

00:00:56.719 --> 00:01:02.120
we devised a timetable, and we've now got
a daily service for this week.

00:01:02.120 --> 00:01:05.860
The B797, the tiny road between Leadhills
in South Lanarkshire

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.720
and Wanlockhead in Dumfries and Galloway,

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:13.500
is shut for resurfacing. The diversion takes
about 45 minutes by road...

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.180
but by luck, there happens to be a volunteer-run,
narrow-gauge railway between the two.

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:23.060
There's all volunteers, there are no paid
employees at Leadhills at all.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:25.240
We all just give up our time for free.

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:28.700
Tomorrow, I'll be the guard,
but today, I'm the driver.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:31.200
And we cover all of the different roles.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.430
It's being used by people
going to the doctor's surgery,

00:01:33.430 --> 00:01:36.350
for people commuting to work
at the Museum of Lead Mining.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:37.790
And a few other places besides.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.820
And yes, it looks a little bit ridiculous.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:42.610
But you know what? It works.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.610
And it's not often you'll find that a disruption
to your journey

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:48.250
can actually make your day better.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:51.780
The response has been great so far this week.

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:55.450
The villagers are enjoying it, and we've also
had a few extra tourists

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.600
that we wouldn't normally get on a weekday
coming to see us.

00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:00.159
So it's good publicity for the line,

00:02:00.159 --> 00:02:01.650
and it helps the villagers!

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.750
Thank you to everyone at the Leadhills and
Wanlockhead railway!

00:02:04.750 --> 00:02:07.650
If you're watching this video on the day or
so after it goes up,

00:02:07.650 --> 00:02:10.090
there may still be time to travel on this
service!

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.780
Details and a link are in the description,

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:14.590
along with the dates when they're running
steam trains on the track this summer.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.520
